{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_014zdMyPYCTgzgp513WVrT5q","type":"tool_result","content":"## R01 G2 Validate every input boundary, including API responses | NA: never\nREQ: List every input the agent receives. That includes API responses (errors and streamed content too), tool and connector output, incoming webhooks and callbacks, messages from other agents, user input, fetched pages, files, and retrieved chunks. Authenticate each sender (prove who sent it), and verify signatures where they exist. Treat content as untrusted even when it comes from an authenticated service or an internal one. Before content reaches the model or runs anywhere, check its type, schema, and size. Also check its content for attack payloads and hidden prompt-injection instructions. Reject or hold aside bad payloads. If a required check fails to run, don't let unchecked content through. Test broken, oversized, faked, and hostile responses from each integration.\nNC: Injected instructions in valid content reaching forbidden tools unblocked->R02 ; Hidden instructions in tool names, descriptions, or schemas->E04 ; Replayed peer-agent requests with no freshness or nonce check->E07 ; Secrets leaking into outputs or normal logs->R03 ; Unapproved sources able to add documents to the retrieval corpus->R04\n - R01.1: Every input path passes through validation code: API and tool responses (including errors and streams), webhooks and callbacks, agent messages, user input, fetched pages, files, and retrieved chunks.\n     V: 1) List every input: tool and API responses (errors, streams), webhooks, agent messages, user input, fetched pages, files, retrieved chunks. 2) Trace each to where it enters the model context. 3) Answer no if any path, including error bodies, stream chunks, or a second handler, reaches the model without passing validation code.\n - R01.2: Type, schema, and size checks run before content, including streamed chunks, is added to the model context or executed.\n     V: 1) Find validation code (`pydantic`, `zod`, `jsonschema`, size limits, `max_bytes`, content-type checks). 2) Check it runs before content is added to the context or executed, including per stream chunk. 3) Answer no if validation runs after use, only on success responses, streams skip it, or there is no size limit.\n - R01.3: Senders are authenticated, and signatures are verified where the integration provides them, including for internal and authenticated services.\n     V: 1) Find webhook, callback, and inbound handlers. 2) Check sender authentication and signature verification (`hmac.compare_digest`, `verify_signature`, mTLS). 3) Answer no if any handler skips it, internal services are trusted without auth, the signature is compared with `==`, or a verify failure is caught and processing continues.\n - R01.4: Content is also screened for attack payloads or injection instructions, not only for structure.\n     V: 1) Find content screening (injection detector, pattern rules, classifier, encoded-payload checks). 2) Check it runs on content, not just schema. 3) Answer no if only type or schema checks exist, or screening covers some inputs (for example user input) but not tool output, fetched pages, or retrieved chunks.\n - R01.5: Bad payloads are rejected or held aside, and if a check errors, times out, or is skipped, the content is blocked.\n     V: 1) Find what happens when validation fails or errors. 2) Read `try`/`except`, `catch`, timeout, and default branches. 3) Answer no if failures log and continue, an error or timeout passes content through, a feature flag can skip checks, or bad content is truncated and still used.\n## R02 G2 Separate data from instructions and test injection containment | NA: never\nREQ: Keep API responses, connector messages, and other outside content out of trusted system and policy channels. Label where each piece came from and how much it is trusted. Test prompt injection in responses that pass schema checks. Include free-text fields, error messages, documents, and tool output. Checks and channel separation can't guarantee that prompt injection is prevented. So show that credential limits, tool permissions, and destructive-action gates still block harm if the model follows an injected instruction. Repeat each attack and include adaptive versions. One failed attempt makes a defense look stronger than it is. B05 covers agents that combine untrusted input, sensitive access, and outside actions.\nNC: Missing type, schema, or size checks on API responses->R01 ; Hidden instructions in tool names, descriptions, or schemas->E04 ; One run planting instructions in another run's memory->R05 ; Generated output run as SQL or shell without parameterizing->R03\n - R02.1: Outside content (API responses, tool output, documents, connector messages) goes into user or tool message roles, never into the system or policy prompt.\n     V: 1) Find where model requests are built (`system=`, `messages`, `role: system`, prompt templates). 2) Trace outside content (tool output, documents, API responses) into the request. 3) Answer no if any outside content is concatenated into the system or policy prompt, including retrieved context or tool descriptions from servers.\n - R02.2: Each piece of outside content carries a source and trust label in the model request.\n     V: 1) Find where outside content is wrapped before the model request. 2) Check each piece carries a source and trust label. 3) Answer no if some sources (for example tool output or retrieved chunks) have no label, or labels are set by the content itself.\n - R02.3: Credential limits, tool permissions, and B05 action gates block the actions an injected instruction could cause, enforced in code the model cannot change.\n     V: 1) List actions an injected instruction could cause: sends, writes, deletes, fetches. 2) Check each is blocked by credential scope, tool allowlist, or B05 gate in harness code. 3) Answer no if any harmful action is stopped only by the model declining, or the limit can be changed by tool arguments or model output.\n - R02.4: Containment does not rely on prompt wording, delimiters, or an injection detector.\n     V: 1) Find the defenses relied on for injection. 2) Check whether removing prompt wording, delimiters, or the detector would leave harmful actions blocked. 3) Answer no if containment depends on system-prompt rules, delimiter tags, or a detector, with no code-level block behind it.\n - R02.5: Injection tests repeat each fixture a set number of times, include adaptive variants and schema-valid payloads, and report success rates.\n     V: 1) Find injection tests (`tests/`, eval suites, red-team fixtures). 2) Check fixtures repeat a set number of times, include adaptive variants and schema-valid payloads, and report a success rate. 3) Answer no if each test runs once, uses only malformed inputs, or reports pass/fail. Answer unknown if tests live outside this repo.\n## R03 G2 Prevent output and log leakage | NA: never\nREQ: Remove secrets and limit sensitive data before tool output, debug output, or responses reach the model, outside recipients, or normal logs. Some clients fetch URLs on their own when they display output, such as Markdown images and link previews. These can leak data without any tool call, so block them or allow only approved URLs. Check generated output before it goes into SQL, a shell, an HTML page, or anything else that runs it. Use parameterized queries and commands (for example, argument lists for shell calls), and the right escaping for each context. Test with fake secrets and injection payloads. Confirm that leaks and unintended execution are blocked, and that redacted audit evidence is kept.\nNC: A tool call sending data to an unapproved destination->B09 ; Long-lived secrets checked into config or baked into images->B03 ; Incoming hostile payloads not validated->R01 ; Retrieval returning another tenant's documents->R04\n - R03.1: Secrets and sensitive fields are redacted from tool output, debug output, and error text before they reach the model, outside recipients, or normal logs.\n     V: 1) Find redaction code (`redact`, `mask`, secret patterns) and where tool output, errors, and debug text go. 2) Check redaction runs before the model, outside recipients, and logs. 3) Answer no if any path skips it, such as error messages, exception traces, debug logging, or `logger.info(response)` before redaction.\n - R03.2: The display client blocks auto-fetched URLs (Markdown images, link previews) or allows only approved hosts.\n     V: 1) Find the display client or renderer (Markdown render, link unfurl, image tags). 2) Check auto-fetched URLs are blocked or limited to approved hosts (CSP `img-src`, URL allowlist). 3) Answer no if Markdown images or previews load any URL. Answer unknown if the display client is outside this repo.\n - R03.3: Generated SQL uses parameterized queries, shell calls use argument lists with no string-built commands, and HTML output is escaped for its context.\n     V: 1) Search for SQL, shell, and HTML built from generated output (`execute(f`, `+ query`, `shell=True`, `exec(`, `innerHTML`, `dangerouslySetInnerHTML`, `|safe`). 2) Check for parameters, argument lists, and context escaping. 3) Answer no if any query or command is string-built, uses `shell=True` or `sh -c` with model input, or HTML is unescaped.\n - R03.4: Redacted audit evidence is kept rather than discarded.\n     V: 1) Find redaction code and audit logging. 2) Check redacted records are written to an audit store. 3) Answer no if redaction drops the whole record, or the evidence is discarded instead of kept.\n## R04 G2 Retrieval-corpus integrity | NA: Only when the agent has no retrieval corpus or search index.\nREQ: The retrieval corpus is the set of documents the agent searches for context. Its index stores those documents, or smaller passages called chunks, in a search engine or vector database. Limit which identities and sources can add or change content there. For each chunk, keep its source, when it was added, its trust level, and its access permissions. Enforce the requesting tenant's access when searching. Test an unapproved attempt to add content and a search across tenants. Also test quarantining, expiring, and revoking access to seeded test documents. Confirm the affected chunks disappear from search results and caches within a stated time. See the [R04 recipe](CHECKLIST-VERIFICATION.md#r--run-time).\nNC: Memory entries poisoned or shared across tenants->R05 ; Agent credentials allowing another tenant's data at the API->B02 ; Retrieved chunks not checked for size, type, or hidden instructions->R01 ; Embedding or reranker model swapped without review->C05\n - R04.1: Only named identities and sources can add or change index content, and the ingestion path checks the writer.\n     V: 1) Find index write paths (`upsert`, `add_documents`, `index`, ingestion jobs). 2) Check each verifies the writer identity or source against a named list. 3) Answer no if any write path, including the agent's own tools or an admin endpoint, accepts writes without that check.\n - R04.2: Each stored chunk has metadata for source, time added, trust level, and access permissions.\n     V: 1) Find chunk write code and metadata fields. 2) Check source, time added, trust level, and access permissions are stored on every chunk. 3) Answer no if any field is missing, or set only on some ingestion paths.\n - R04.3: Search applies the requesting tenant's access filter at query time, in the store or retrieval code, not after the model answers.\n     V: 1) Find search calls (`similarity_search`, `query`, `search`, `filter=`, `where=`). 2) Check a tenant or access filter is applied in the store query, from the caller's identity. 3) Answer no if filtering happens after retrieval or in the prompt, the filter is optional, or the tenant comes from a model-supplied argument.\n - R04.4: Code can quarantine, expire, and revoke chunks, and it also clears or invalidates search caches.\n     V: 1) Find quarantine, expiry, and revoke code (`delete`, `quarantine`, `ttl`, `expires_at`). 2) Check search excludes those chunks and caches are cleared. 3) Answer no if any of the three operations is missing, or caches (query cache, embedding cache) are not invalidated.\n## R05 G3 Memory isolation and write validation | NA: Only when the agent has no memory that lasts between runs.\nREQ: Separate memory by tenant, agent type, instance, and task as needed. Block access across those lines unless it is clearly approved. Check what gets written to memory. Test that one poisoned run can't quietly plant instructions in another run's memory. Don't move the agent's own output into trusted memory without review, and let unverified entries expire. Write checks must also cover content that arrives through ordinary queries, not just special write paths.\nNC: No record of who wrote a memory entry, or no way to remove it->R06 ; Retrieval search not filtered by the requesting tenant->R04 ; Agent credentials allowing another tenant's data at the API->B02 ; Rollback restoring old memory or index state unchecked->C07\n - R05.1: Memory reads and writes are scoped by tenant, agent type, instance, and task as needed, and cross-scope access is denied unless explicitly allowed.\n     V: 1) Find memory read and write code (`memory`, `store`, `namespace`, keys). 2) Check keys include tenant, agent type, instance, or task as needed. 3) Answer no if keys omit tenant, the scope comes from a model argument, or reads fall back to a shared or global namespace.\n - R05.2: Every memory write, including content arriving through ordinary queries, passes validation before it is stored.\n     V: 1) List every memory write path, including automatic saves from ordinary conversation. 2) Check each passes validation before storing. 3) Answer no if any path writes without validation, or validation errors are caught and the write continues.\n - R05.3: The agent's own output cannot reach trusted or shared memory without a review step.\n     V: 1) Find where agent output is written to shared or trusted memory. 2) Check a review step gates the write. 3) Answer no if agent output goes straight into shared or trusted memory, or the review flag defaults to approved.\n - R05.4: Unverified entries have an expiry that is enforced.\n     V: 1) Find how unverified entries are marked and expired (`ttl`, `expires_at`, cleanup jobs). 2) Check reads filter out expired entries. 3) Answer no if no expiry is set, it is set but never enforced on read or by a job, or the default is no expiry.\n## R06 G3 Memory provenance and cleanup | NA: Only when the agent has no memory that lasts between runs.\nREQ: For any memory entry, look up who wrote it, when, for which task, from what input, and the context size at the time. Show that you can find, quarantine, and remove bad entries, and control their reuse in later sessions.\nNC: Memory not separated by tenant, agent type, instance, or task->R05 ; Poisoned retrieval documents not quarantined or expired->R04 ; Context size not logged for each action decision->R07 ; Prompt a parent gave a sub-agent not recorded->A06\n - R06.1: Each memory entry stores its writer, time, task, source input, and context size at write time.\n     V: 1) Find the memory entry schema. 2) Check fields for writer, time, task, source input, and context size. 3) Answer no if any is missing, or set only on some write paths.\n - R06.2: Derived entries such as summaries keep links to the entries they came from.\n     V: 1) Find summary or derived-entry creation code. 2) Check it stores links to source entry IDs. 3) Answer no if derived entries keep no source links.\n - R06.3: Code can find entries by source or writer and quarantine or delete them, along with derived entries and caches.\n     V: 1) Find code that queries by source or writer and quarantines or deletes. 2) Check it also handles derived entries and caches. 3) Answer no if lookup by source or writer is impossible, or derived entries and caches are left in place.\n - R06.4: Quarantined or deleted entries are excluded from retrieval in later sessions.\n     V: 1) Find memory retrieval code. 2) Check it excludes quarantined or deleted entries in new sessions. 3) Answer no if retrieval ignores the quarantine flag, or a cache or index still returns removed entries.\n## R07 G2 Decision-time context size | NA: never\nREQ: For each action, log how full the model's context was when it decided, in tokens. Document how you count. Say whether each value was measured, estimated, or unavailable. Cover cached input, compaction (shrinking the context), and limits the provider hides. Per-call billing totals don't automatically equal context size. Use them only if your counting method shows they match. A missing value is a clear gap, never zero. Check that the value survives tool handoffs and can be searched next to the action's identity and result. See the [telemetry conventions](otel-conventions.md).\nNC: Behavior not compared across context-size ranges->R09 ; Token or cost limits not enforced outside the model->B07 ; Action records missing any of the six identity fields->A02\n - R07.1: Each action record includes the model's context size in tokens at decision time.\n     V: 1) Find action or tool-call records (trace spans, audit logs). 2) Check each has context size in tokens at decision time. 3) Answer no if the field is missing or recorded only per run, not per action.\n - R07.2: Each value is labeled measured, estimated, or unavailable, and a missing value is stored as unknown, never as zero.\n     V: 1) Find where context size is recorded. 2) Check a label of measured, estimated, or unavailable, and how missing values are stored. 3) Answer no if there is no label, or a missing value defaults to `0` (`or 0`, `?? 0`, `.get(..., 0)`).\n - R07.3: The count comes from a stated method that handles cached input and compaction, not a copied per-call billing total.\n     V: 1) Find how context size is counted. 2) Check a stated method covers cached input and compaction. 3) Answer no if it copies `usage.input_tokens` or a billing total with no stated method that matches it, or ignores cache and compaction.\n - R07.4: The value is carried across tool handoffs and stored with the action's identity, result, and trace ID.\n     V: 1) Trace context size through tool handoffs and sub-agent calls. 2) Check it is stored with action identity, result, and trace ID. 3) Answer no if it is lost on handoff, or stored separately with no link to the action.\n   14185 .r2.txt","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e7e91a56-8e26-4882-b939-10679f6422bd","uuid":"b8386d48-3090-4888-a6c7-06bb4b2ec535","timestamp":"2026-10-02T14:35:24.889Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"## R01 G2 Validate every input boundary, including API responses | NA: never\nREQ: List every input the agent receives. That includes API responses (errors and streamed content too), tool and connector output, incoming webhooks and callbacks, messages from other agents, user input, fetched pages, files, and retrieved chunks. Authenticate each sender (prove who sent it), and verify signatures where they exist. Treat content as untrusted even when it comes from an authenticated service or an internal one. Before content reaches the model or runs anywhere, check its type, schema, and size. Also check its content for attack payloads and hidden prompt-injection instructions. Reject or hold aside bad payloads. If a required check fails to run, don't let unchecked content through. Test broken, oversized, faked, and hostile responses from each integration.\nNC: Injected instructions in valid content reaching forbidden tools unblocked->R02 ; Hidden instructions in tool names, descriptions, or schemas->E04 ; Replayed peer-agent requests with no freshness or nonce check->E07 ; Secrets leaking into outputs or normal logs->R03 ; Unapproved sources able to add documents to the retrieval corpus->R04\n - R01.1: Every input path passes through validation code: API and tool responses (including errors and streams), webhooks and callbacks, agent messages, user input, fetched pages, files, and retrieved chunks.\n     V: 1) List every input: tool and API responses (errors, streams), webhooks, agent messages, user input, fetched pages, files, retrieved chunks. 2) Trace each to where it enters the model context. 3) Answer no if any path, including error bodies, stream chunks, or a second handler, reaches the model without passing validation code.\n - R01.2: Type, schema, and size checks run before content, including streamed chunks, is added to the model context or executed.\n     V: 1) Find validation code (`pydantic`, `zod`, `jsonschema`, size limits, `max_bytes`, content-type checks). 2) Check it runs before content is added to the context or executed, including per stream chunk. 3) Answer no if validation runs after use, only on success responses, streams skip it, or there is no size limit.\n - R01.3: Senders are authenticated, and signatures are verified where the integration provides them, including for internal and authenticated services.\n     V: 1) Find webhook, callback, and inbound handlers. 2) Check sender authentication and signature verification (`hmac.compare_digest`, `verify_signature`, mTLS). 3) Answer no if any handler skips it, internal services are trusted without auth, the signature is compared with `==`, or a verify failure is caught and processing continues.\n - R01.4: Content is also screened for attack payloads or injection instructions, not only for structure.\n     V: 1) Find content screening (injection detector, pattern rules, classifier, encoded-payload checks). 2) Check it runs on content, not just schema. 3) Answer no if only type or schema checks exist, or screening covers some inputs (for example user input) but not tool output, fetched pages, or retrieved chunks.\n - R01.5: Bad payloads are rejected or held aside, and if a check errors, times out, or is skipped, the content is blocked.\n     V: 1) Find what happens when validation fails or errors. 2) Read `try`/`except`, `catch`, timeout, and default branches. 3) Answer no if failures log and continue, an error or timeout passes content through, a feature flag can skip checks, or bad content is truncated and still used.\n## R02 G2 Separate data from instructions and test injection containment | NA: never\nREQ: Keep API responses, connector messages, and other outside content out of trusted system and policy channels. Label where each piece came from and how much it is trusted. Test prompt injection in responses that pass schema checks. Include free-text fields, error messages, documents, and tool output. Checks and channel separation can't guarantee that prompt injection is prevented. So show that credential limits, tool permissions, and destructive-action gates still block harm if the model follows an injected instruction. Repeat each attack and include adaptive versions. One failed attempt makes a defense look stronger than it is. B05 covers agents that combine untrusted input, sensitive access, and outside actions.\nNC: Missing type, schema, or size checks on API responses->R01 ; Hidden instructions in tool names, descriptions, or schemas->E04 ; One run planting instructions in another run's memory->R05 ; Generated output run as SQL or shell without parameterizing->R03\n - R02.1: Outside content (API responses, tool output, documents, connector messages) goes into user or tool message roles, never into the system or policy prompt.\n     V: 1) Find where model requests are built (`system=`, `messages`, `role: system`, prompt templates). 2) Trace outside content (tool output, documents, API responses) into the request. 3) Answer no if any outside content is concatenated into the system or policy prompt, including retrieved context or tool descriptions from servers.\n - R02.2: Each piece of outside content carries a source and trust label in the model request.\n     V: 1) Find where outside content is wrapped before the model request. 2) Check each piece carries a source and trust label. 3) Answer no if some sources (for example tool output or retrieved chunks) have no label, or labels are set by the content itself.\n - R02.3: Credential limits, tool permissions, and B05 action gates block the actions an injected instruction could cause, enforced in code the model cannot change.\n     V: 1) List actions an injected instruction could cause: sends, writes, deletes, fetches. 2) Check each is blocked by credential scope, tool allowlist, or B05 gate in harness code. 3) Answer no if any harmful action is stopped only by the model declining, or the limit can be changed by tool arguments or model output.\n - R02.4: Containment does not rely on prompt wording, delimiters, or an injection detector.\n     V: 1) Find the defenses relied on for injection. 2) Check whether removing prompt wording, delimiters, or the detector would leave harmful actions blocked. 3) Answer no if containment depends on system-prompt rules, delimiter tags, or a detector, with no code-level block behind it.\n - R02.5: Injection tests repeat each fixture a set number of times, include adaptive variants and schema-valid payloads, and report success rates.\n     V: 1) Find injection tests (`tests/`, eval suites, red-team fixtures). 2) Check fixtures repeat a set number of times, include adaptive variants and schema-valid payloads, and report a success rate. 3) Answer no if each test runs once, uses only malformed inputs, or reports pass/fail. Answer unknown if tests live outside this repo.\n## R03 G2 Prevent output and log leakage | NA: never\nREQ: Remove secrets and limit sensitive data before tool output, debug output, or responses reach the model, outside recipients, or normal logs. Some clients fetch URLs on their own when they display output, such as Markdown images and link previews. These can leak data without any tool call, so block them or allow only approved URLs. Check generated output before it goes into SQL, a shell, an HTML page, or anything else that runs it. Use parameterized queries and commands (for example, argument lists for shell calls), and the right escaping for each context. Test with fake secrets and injection payloads. Confirm that leaks and unintended execution are blocked, and that redacted audit evidence is kept.\nNC: A tool call sending data to an unapproved destination->B09 ; Long-lived secrets checked into config or baked into images->B03 ; Incoming hostile payloads not validated->R01 ; Retrieval returning another tenant's documents->R04\n - R03.1: Secrets and sensitive fields are redacted from tool output, debug output, and error text before they reach the model, outside recipients, or normal logs.\n     V: 1) Find redaction code (`redact`, `mask`, secret patterns) and where tool output, errors, and debug text go. 2) Check redaction runs before the model, outside recipients, and logs. 3) Answer no if any path skips it, such as error messages, exception traces, debug logging, or `logger.info(response)` before redaction.\n - R03.2: The display client blocks auto-fetched URLs (Markdown images, link previews) or allows only approved hosts.\n     V: 1) Find the display client or renderer (Markdown render, link unfurl, image tags). 2) Check auto-fetched URLs are blocked or limited to approved hosts (CSP `img-src`, URL allowlist). 3) Answer no if Markdown images or previews load any URL. Answer unknown if the display client is outside this repo.\n - R03.3: Generated SQL uses parameterized queries, shell calls use argument lists with no string-built commands, and HTML output is escaped for its context.\n     V: 1) Search for SQL, shell, and HTML built from generated output (`execute(f`, `+ query`, `shell=True`, `exec(`, `innerHTML`, `dangerouslySetInnerHTML`, `|safe`). 2) Check for parameters, argument lists, and context escaping. 3) Answer no if any query or command is string-built, uses `shell=True` or `sh -c` with model input, or HTML is unescaped.\n - R03.4: Redacted audit evidence is kept rather than discarded.\n     V: 1) Find redaction code and audit logging. 2) Check redacted records are written to an audit store. 3) Answer no if redaction drops the whole record, or the evidence is discarded instead of kept.\n## R04 G2 Retrieval-corpus integrity | NA: Only when the agent has no retrieval corpus or search index.\nREQ: The retrieval corpus is the set of documents the agent searches for context. Its index stores those documents, or smaller passages called chunks, in a search engine or vector database. Limit which identities and sources can add or change content there. For each chunk, keep its source, when it was added, its trust level, and its access permissions. Enforce the requesting tenant's access when searching. Test an unapproved attempt to add content and a search across tenants. Also test quarantining, expiring, and revoking access to seeded test documents. Confirm the affected chunks disappear from search results and caches within a stated time. See the [R04 recipe](CHECKLIST-VERIFICATION.md#r--run-time).\nNC: Memory entries poisoned or shared across tenants->R05 ; Agent credentials allowing another tenant's data at the API->B02 ; Retrieved chunks not checked for size, type, or hidden instructions->R01 ; Embedding or reranker model swapped without review->C05\n - R04.1: Only named identities and sources can add or change index content, and the ingestion path checks the writer.\n     V: 1) Find index write paths (`upsert`, `add_documents`, `index`, ingestion jobs). 2) Check each verifies the writer identity or source against a named list. 3) Answer no if any write path, including the agent's own tools or an admin endpoint, accepts writes without that check.\n - R04.2: Each stored chunk has metadata for source, time added, trust level, and access permissions.\n     V: 1) Find chunk write code and metadata fields. 2) Check source, time added, trust level, and access permissions are stored on every chunk. 3) Answer no if any field is missing, or set only on some ingestion paths.\n - R04.3: Search applies the requesting tenant's access filter at query time, in the store or retrieval code, not after the model answers.\n     V: 1) Find search calls (`similarity_search`, `query`, `search`, `filter=`, `where=`). 2) Check a tenant or access filter is applied in the store query, from the caller's identity. 3) Answer no if filtering happens after retrieval or in the prompt, the filter is optional, or the tenant comes from a model-supplied argument.\n - R04.4: Code can quarantine, expire, and revoke chunks, and it also clears or invalidates search caches.\n     V: 1) Find quarantine, expiry, and revoke code (`delete`, `quarantine`, `ttl`, `expires_at`). 2) Check search excludes those chunks and caches are cleared. 3) Answer no if any of the three operations is missing, or caches (query cache, embedding cache) are not invalidated.\n## R05 G3 Memory isolation and write validation | NA: Only when the agent has no memory that lasts between runs.\nREQ: Separate memory by tenant, agent type, instance, and task as needed. Block access across those lines unless it is clearly approved. Check what gets written to memory. Test that one poisoned run can't quietly plant instructions in another run's memory. Don't move the agent's own output into trusted memory without review, and let unverified entries expire. Write checks must also cover content that arrives through ordinary queries, not just special write paths.\nNC: No record of who wrote a memory entry, or no way to remove it->R06 ; Retrieval search not filtered by the requesting tenant->R04 ; Agent credentials allowing another tenant's data at the API->B02 ; Rollback restoring old memory or index state unchecked->C07\n - R05.1: Memory reads and writes are scoped by tenant, agent type, instance, and task as needed, and cross-scope access is denied unless explicitly allowed.\n     V: 1) Find memory read and write code (`memory`, `store`, `namespace`, keys). 2) Check keys include tenant, agent type, instance, or task as needed. 3) Answer no if keys omit tenant, the scope comes from a model argument, or reads fall back to a shared or global namespace.\n - R05.2: Every memory write, including content arriving through ordinary queries, passes validation before it is stored.\n     V: 1) List every memory write path, including automatic saves from ordinary conversation. 2) Check each passes validation before storing. 3) Answer no if any path writes without validation, or validation errors are caught and the write continues.\n - R05.3: The agent's own output cannot reach trusted or shared memory without a review step.\n     V: 1) Find where agent output is written to shared or trusted memory. 2) Check a review step gates the write. 3) Answer no if agent output goes straight into shared or trusted memory, or the review flag defaults to approved.\n - R05.4: Unverified entries have an expiry that is enforced.\n     V: 1) Find how unverified entries are marked and expired (`ttl`, `expires_at`, cleanup jobs). 2) Check reads filter out expired entries. 3) Answer no if no expiry is set, it is set but never enforced on read or by a job, or the default is no expiry.\n## R06 G3 Memory provenance and cleanup | NA: Only when the agent has no memory that lasts between runs.\nREQ: For any memory entry, look up who wrote it, when, for which task, from what input, and the context size at the time. Show that you can find, quarantine, and remove bad entries, and control their reuse in later sessions.\nNC: Memory not separated by tenant, agent type, instance, or task->R05 ; Poisoned retrieval documents not quarantined or expired->R04 ; Context size not logged for each action decision->R07 ; Prompt a parent gave a sub-agent not recorded->A06\n - R06.1: Each memory entry stores its writer, time, task, source input, and context size at write time.\n     V: 1) Find the memory entry schema. 2) Check fields for writer, time, task, source input, and context size. 3) Answer no if any is missing, or set only on some write paths.\n - R06.2: Derived entries such as summaries keep links to the entries they came from.\n     V: 1) Find summary or derived-entry creation code. 2) Check it stores links to source entry IDs. 3) Answer no if derived entries keep no source links.\n - R06.3: Code can find entries by source or writer and quarantine or delete them, along with derived entries and caches.\n     V: 1) Find code that queries by source or writer and quarantines or deletes. 2) Check it also handles derived entries and caches. 3) Answer no if lookup by source or writer is impossible, or derived entries and caches are left in place.\n - R06.4: Quarantined or deleted entries are excluded from retrieval in later sessions.\n     V: 1) Find memory retrieval code. 2) Check it excludes quarantined or deleted entries in new sessions. 3) Answer no if retrieval ignores the quarantine flag, or a cache or index still returns removed entries.\n## R07 G2 Decision-time context size | NA: never\nREQ: For each action, log how full the model's context was when it decided, in tokens. Document how you count. Say whether each value was measured, estimated, or unavailable. Cover cached input, compaction (shrinking the context), and limits the provider hides. Per-call billing totals don't automatically equal context size. Use them only if your counting method shows they match. A missing value is a clear gap, never zero. Check that the value survives tool handoffs and can be searched next to the action's identity and result. See the [telemetry conventions](otel-conventions.md).\nNC: Behavior not compared across context-size ranges->R09 ; Token or cost limits not enforced outside the model->B07 ; Action records missing any of the six identity fields->A02\n - R07.1: Each action record includes the model's context size in tokens at decision time.\n     V: 1) Find action or tool-call records (trace spans, audit logs). 2) Check each has context size in tokens at decision time. 3) Answer no if the field is missing or recorded only per run, not per action.\n - R07.2: Each value is labeled measured, estimated, or unavailable, and a missing value is stored as unknown, never as zero.\n     V: 1) Find where context size is recorded. 2) Check a label of measured, estimated, or unavailable, and how missing values are stored. 3) Answer no if there is no label, or a missing value defaults to `0` (`or 0`, `?? 0`, `.get(..., 0)`).\n - R07.3: The count comes from a stated method that handles cached input and compaction, not a copied per-call billing total.\n     V: 1) Find how context size is counted. 2) Check a stated method covers cached input and compaction. 3) Answer no if it copies `usage.input_tokens` or a billing total with no stated method that matches it, or ignores cache and compaction.\n - R07.4: The value is carried across tool handoffs and stored with the action's identity, result, and trace ID.\n     V: 1) Trace context size through tool handoffs and sub-agent calls. 2) Check it is stored with action identity, result, and trace ID. 3) Answer no if it is lost on handoff, or stored separately with no link to the action.\n   14185 .r2.txt","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false}}
