{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CfdaStpFgUS6qLRH1rXSs","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01VPX2VG81SEwigvgwMJX5x9","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cat .r2.txt","description":"Show R08-R14 items"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6558,"cache_read_input_tokens":119129,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":6558},"output_tokens":9,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e7e91a56-8e26-4882-b939-10679f6422bd","uuid":"1ff81078-a675-4248-a8e5-0b2dbbc0d242","timestamp":"2026-10-02T14:35:45.681Z","request_id":"req_011CfdaStTfxHnNWEg2CgDk1","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01VPX2VG81SEwigvgwMJX5x9":{"command":"cd /private/var/folders/6v/yk9bzvrn5zx0kxll7d4szzb80000gn/T/brace-repeat-kcdj20d6/skill && cat .r2.txt","description":"Show R08-R14 items"}},"wire_ingest_context":{"toolu_01VPX2VG81SEwigvgwMJX5x9":{"cwd":"/private/var/folders/6v/yk9bzvrn5zx0kxll7d4szzb80000gn/T/brace-repeat-kcdj20d6/skill"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01VPX2VG81SEwigvgwMJX5x9","type":"tool_result","content":"## R08 G3 Security monitoring | NA: never\nREQ: Keep security rules and baselines separate from answer-quality monitoring. Test encoded payloads, unexpected outbound traffic, writes outside scope, privilege escalation, and repeated denied calls. Check that alerts reach a named responder.\nNC: Chains of individually allowed actions going undetected->R09 ; No test of a second-model review against evasive behavior->R10 ; No drill for a compromised shared tool or agent type->E12 ; Rate and cost limits not enforced outside the model->B07\n - R08.1: Security detection rules and baselines are defined separately from answer-quality monitoring.\n     V: 1) Find detection rules and monitoring config (SIEM rules, alert config, eval dashboards). 2) Check security rules are separate from answer-quality metrics. 3) Answer no if only quality monitoring exists. Answer unknown if monitoring lives outside this repo.\n - R08.2: Rules exist for encoded payloads, unexpected outbound traffic, out-of-scope writes, privilege escalation, and repeated denied calls.\n     V: 1) Read the security detection rules. 2) Check for encoded payloads, unexpected outbound traffic, out-of-scope writes, privilege escalation, and repeated denied calls. 3) Answer no if any is missing, or denied calls are never logged so the rule cannot fire.\n - R08.3: Alerts from these rules route to a named responder or on-call target in config.\n     V: 1) Find alert routing (PagerDuty, Opsgenie, alertmanager receivers, on-call config). 2) Check the security rules route to a named responder. 3) Answer no if alerts go only to a dashboard or log, or the receiver is empty. Answer unknown if routing lives outside this repo.\n## R09 G3 Sequence and context-aware detection | NA: never\nREQ: Test suspicious chains of actions that are each allowed on their own. Also test unusual fan-out and resource use. Compare behavior across context-size ranges, so one fleet-wide baseline doesn't hide failures in long contexts. If the agent could be hijacked while using only allowed tools, check each high-impact action against the original task before it runs. Use a checker that doesn't see the untrusted content in between, and measure what it misses.\nNC: Encoded payloads or writes outside scope going undetected->R08 ; No test against a deliberately evasive, misaligned agent->R10 ; Context size not logged for each action decision->R07\n - R09.1: Detection rules evaluate sequences of calls across a run (for example many reads followed by an outside send), not only single calls.\n     V: 1) Read detection rules. 2) Check rules evaluate sequences across a run, such as many reads then an outside send. 3) Answer no if every rule looks at a single call or event.\n - R09.2: Rules cover unusual fan-out and resource use.\n     V: 1) Read detection rules. 2) Check rules for unusual fan-out (sub-agents, parallel calls) and resource use. 3) Answer no if neither is covered.\n - R09.3: Thresholds or baselines are set per context-size range, not as one fleet-wide value.\n     V: 1) Read rule thresholds and baselines. 2) Check they are set per context-size range. 3) Answer no if one fleet-wide value is used for all contexts.\n - R09.4: If hijack through allowed tools alone is possible, each high-impact action is checked against the original task before it runs, by a checker that is not given the untrusted content.\n     V: 1) Check whether allowed tools alone could cause harm if the agent is hijacked. 2) If so, find a task-alignment checker before high-impact actions and see what input it receives. 3) Answer no if no checker exists, it is given the untrusted content, or its errors let the action proceed.\n## R10 G3 Misaligned-behavior review | NA: Only when deliberate misalignment is recorded as out of scope for this deployment, with a reason and approval.\nREQ: If deliberate misalignment is in scope, test a second model or pattern review against realistic evasive behavior. Record what it can and can't catch. Include what the reviewer can see: actions, arguments, and any visible reasoning. Reasoning the reviewer can't see is a stated gap. Include red-team runs by an agent that knows it is being watched. This review must not replace rule-based access and action controls that don't depend on a model's judgment. Deeper checks on intent belong in a separate AI-control program at the model level.\nNC: Destructive actions not blocked by a rule-based gate->B05 ; A swapped, drifting, or failing checker model not treated as a failure->A07 ; Chains of individually allowed actions going undetected->R09\n## R11 G1 Tested kill switch | NA: never\nREQ: Name who may stop the agent and how. The stop path must not depend on the agent's cooperation. The agent's credentials must not be able to change it. Set a maximum stop time that fits the potential harm. Run a dated drill on the current deployment. It must stop new actions, running work, and open tool streams, subscriptions, and tasks. Include an attempt by the agent to disable or delay the stop. Cover sub-agents and their children in the ecosystem drill (E09).\nNC: Stop signal not reaching sub-agents, children, or remote workers->E09 ; In-flight writes, queued actions, or unattended restarts after the stop->R12 ; No way to cut off the agent's access without cutting off the user->B04\n - R11.1: A stop control halts new model calls and new tool calls when set.\n     V: 1) Find the stop control (kill flag, `stop`, `halt`, feature flag, revoked token). 2) Check the loop checks it before every model call and tool call. 3) Answer no if it is checked only at run start, only between turns, a second loop or worker skips it, or a check error is treated as not stopped.\n - R11.2: The agent's own credentials and tools cannot set, clear, delete, or delay the stop control.\n     V: 1) Find where the stop flag is stored and who can write it. 2) Compare with the agent's credentials and tools. 3) Answer no if the agent's role can write, delete, or expire the flag, a tool can reach its store, or the agent can delay it (for example by catching the stop signal).\n - R11.3: Tool calls already running are cancelled or bounded by a timeout when the stop is set.\n     V: 1) Find the stop handler. 2) Check running tool calls are cancelled or bounded by a timeout. 3) Answer no if running calls finish with no timeout, or cancellation errors are swallowed and the call continues.\n - R11.4: The stop also closes open tool streams, subscriptions, and background tasks.\n     V: 1) Find streams, subscriptions, and background tasks (`asyncio.create_task`, goroutines, websockets, SSE). 2) Check the stop handler closes them. 3) Answer no if any is left open, detached, or untracked.\n - R11.5: Only named operator identities can set the stop control.\n     V: 1) Find who can set the stop (auth on the endpoint, IAM on the flag store). 2) Answer no if any authenticated user or service can set it with no named operator list. Answer unknown if access lives outside this repo.\n## R12 G1 Safe state after stopping | NA: never\nREQ: Decide what happens to writes in progress, queued actions, and outside side effects. Where you can't cancel something, show how you check and fix the result (reconcile) or undo it another way (compensate). No restart may happen unattended, and no orphaned work may be left able to act.\nNC: Stop path depends on the agent or has no tested stop time->R11 ; Recovery replay repeating irreversible actions or using tampered checkpoints->R14 ; Rollback restoring revoked permissions->C07\n - R12.1: For each operation type, code or config states whether it can be cancelled, completed, or only reconciled after a stop.\n     V: 1) Search code and config for a per-operation stop policy (cancel, complete, reconcile). 2) Answer no if any operation type the agent can run has no stated stop behavior.\n - R12.2: On stop, queued actions are cancelled or held, and automatic retries are turned off.\n     V: 1) Find queue and retry code (`retry`, `backoff`, queue workers). 2) Check stop cancels or holds queued items and turns retries off. 3) Answer no if retries continue after stop or queued jobs still run.\n - R12.3: Unclear or in-progress outside actions go on a tracked hold with an owner, and dependent actions are blocked until reconciled.\n     V: 1) Find handling of unclear or in-progress outside actions at stop. 2) Check they go on a tracked hold with an owner and block dependent actions. 3) Answer no if they are dropped, retried, or dependents proceed.\n - R12.4: A stopped run does not restart on its own; restart needs an operator action.\n     V: 1) Find restart logic (supervisors, `restartPolicy`, cron, auto-resume). 2) Answer no if a stopped run restarts automatically (`restartPolicy: Always`, auto-resume from checkpoint) without an operator action.\n - R12.5: Workers and queued jobs lose their credentials or authority at stop, so no orphaned work can act.\n     V: 1) Find workers and queued jobs and the credentials they hold. 2) Check stop revokes or expires them. 3) Answer no if workers keep long-lived credentials after stop, or jobs carry their own tokens that stay valid.\n## R13 G1 Full execution audit | NA: never\nREQ: Record each run as an execution graph: a linked record of every step. It must include proposed actions, tool calls and key arguments, and authorization decisions with the policy version used. It must also include denials, approvals with their IDs, results, errors, and timestamps. Rebuild a typical run from this record, including failed actions. Don't rely on the run's final answer.\nNC: Action records missing any of the six identity fields->A02 ; Agent able to erase or rewrite its own audit history->E10 ; Audit retention, access, redaction, or export rules missing->E11 ; Run can't be rebuilt across gateway, tool services, and delegated workers->E10 ; Prompt a parent gave a sub-agent not recorded->A06\n - R13.1: Each step writes a record linked to the run: proposed action, tool call with key arguments, result, error, and timestamp.\n     V: 1) Find step logging (trace spans, audit log writes). 2) Check each record has run ID, proposed action, tool call with key arguments, result, error, and timestamp. 3) Answer no if any field is missing, or logging happens only on the success branch.\n - R13.2: Authorization decisions are recorded with the policy version, including denials, not only allowed calls.\n     V: 1) Find authorization decision code. 2) Check both allow and deny are logged with the policy version. 3) Answer no if denials are not logged, or no policy version is recorded.\n - R13.3: Approved actions record their approval ID.\n     V: 1) Find where approved actions are logged. 2) Answer no if the approval ID is not recorded with the action.\n - R13.4: Tool errors, timeouts, and retries are recorded, and each operation gets an ID before it is sent, so unclear results can be matched to the destination's records.\n     V: 1) Find error, timeout, and retry handling. 2) Check each is logged and each operation gets an ID before sending. 3) Answer no if `except`/`catch` blocks skip logging, retries are not logged, or the ID is created after the response.\n - R13.5: Records are written by the harness or gateway, not by the model, and do not depend on the run's final answer.\n     V: 1) Find who writes records. 2) Answer no if the model writes them via a tool, records are built from the final answer, or logging runs only if the run completes.\n## R14 G2 Recovery integrity | NA: Only when no logs or checkpoints drive recovery and there is no automatic replay.\nREQ: If logs or checkpoints drive recovery, make them tamper-evident. Before using them, verify them against a separately protected trust anchor or signing key. Test that verification rejects changed checkpoints. It must also catch truncation, rollback to an older valid checkpoint, and replacement of a whole hash chain. For automatic recovery, confirm the destination supports idempotency: sending the same request twice has the effect of sending it once. Replay only within the destination's documented scope and time window. Confirm that actions you can't undo don't happen twice. Where support is missing or the result is unclear, check that replay pauses for reconciliation instead of sending again.\nNC: What happens to in-flight writes and queued actions at stop->R12 ; Rollback to an approved earlier release not tested->C07 ; Agent able to delete or rewrite its own audit history->E10\n - R14.1: Checkpoints and audit records used for recovery are hash-chained or signed.\n     V: 1) Find checkpoint and audit storage used for recovery. 2) Check for hash chaining or signatures. 3) Answer no if records are plain rows or files with no chain or signature.\n - R14.2: Recovery verifies them against a key or trust anchor stored separately from the records, and refuses any that fail.\n     V: 1) Find recovery code that loads checkpoints. 2) Check it verifies against a key stored separately and refuses failures. 3) Answer no if the key sits in the same store, verification is skipped, or failure is logged and recovery continues.\n - R14.3: Verification catches truncation, rollback to an older valid checkpoint, and replacement of the whole chain, for example with a signed head or sequence number held outside the store.\n     V: 1) Check verification uses a signed head or sequence number held outside the store. 2) Answer no if truncation, rollback to an older valid checkpoint, or replacing the whole chain would still pass.\n - R14.4: Automatic replay sends idempotency keys that the destination enforces, and only for integrations that support them.\n     V: 1) Find replay code. 2) Check it sends idempotency keys (`Idempotency-Key`, `client_token`) only to integrations that enforce them. 3) Answer no if replay sends without keys, uses a local dedupe table as the only guard, or replays to integrations without support.\n - R14.5: When idempotency is missing or the result is unclear, replay pauses for reconciliation instead of resending.\n     V: 1) Find replay handling for unclear results or missing idempotency. 2) Answer no if replay resends instead of pausing for reconciliation, or retry is the default on timeout.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e7e91a56-8e26-4882-b939-10679f6422bd","uuid":"3758369e-0644-481a-9547-a75465a86cf0","timestamp":"2026-10-02T14:35:45.714Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"## R08 G3 Security monitoring | NA: never\nREQ: Keep security rules and baselines separate from answer-quality monitoring. Test encoded payloads, unexpected outbound traffic, writes outside scope, privilege escalation, and repeated denied calls. Check that alerts reach a named responder.\nNC: Chains of individually allowed actions going undetected->R09 ; No test of a second-model review against evasive behavior->R10 ; No drill for a compromised shared tool or agent type->E12 ; Rate and cost limits not enforced outside the model->B07\n - R08.1: Security detection rules and baselines are defined separately from answer-quality monitoring.\n     V: 1) Find detection rules and monitoring config (SIEM rules, alert config, eval dashboards). 2) Check security rules are separate from answer-quality metrics. 3) Answer no if only quality monitoring exists. Answer unknown if monitoring lives outside this repo.\n - R08.2: Rules exist for encoded payloads, unexpected outbound traffic, out-of-scope writes, privilege escalation, and repeated denied calls.\n     V: 1) Read the security detection rules. 2) Check for encoded payloads, unexpected outbound traffic, out-of-scope writes, privilege escalation, and repeated denied calls. 3) Answer no if any is missing, or denied calls are never logged so the rule cannot fire.\n - R08.3: Alerts from these rules route to a named responder or on-call target in config.\n     V: 1) Find alert routing (PagerDuty, Opsgenie, alertmanager receivers, on-call config). 2) Check the security rules route to a named responder. 3) Answer no if alerts go only to a dashboard or log, or the receiver is empty. Answer unknown if routing lives outside this repo.\n## R09 G3 Sequence and context-aware detection | NA: never\nREQ: Test suspicious chains of actions that are each allowed on their own. Also test unusual fan-out and resource use. Compare behavior across context-size ranges, so one fleet-wide baseline doesn't hide failures in long contexts. If the agent could be hijacked while using only allowed tools, check each high-impact action against the original task before it runs. Use a checker that doesn't see the untrusted content in between, and measure what it misses.\nNC: Encoded payloads or writes outside scope going undetected->R08 ; No test against a deliberately evasive, misaligned agent->R10 ; Context size not logged for each action decision->R07\n - R09.1: Detection rules evaluate sequences of calls across a run (for example many reads followed by an outside send), not only single calls.\n     V: 1) Read detection rules. 2) Check rules evaluate sequences across a run, such as many reads then an outside send. 3) Answer no if every rule looks at a single call or event.\n - R09.2: Rules cover unusual fan-out and resource use.\n     V: 1) Read detection rules. 2) Check rules for unusual fan-out (sub-agents, parallel calls) and resource use. 3) Answer no if neither is covered.\n - R09.3: Thresholds or baselines are set per context-size range, not as one fleet-wide value.\n     V: 1) Read rule thresholds and baselines. 2) Check they are set per context-size range. 3) Answer no if one fleet-wide value is used for all contexts.\n - R09.4: If hijack through allowed tools alone is possible, each high-impact action is checked against the original task before it runs, by a checker that is not given the untrusted content.\n     V: 1) Check whether allowed tools alone could cause harm if the agent is hijacked. 2) If so, find a task-alignment checker before high-impact actions and see what input it receives. 3) Answer no if no checker exists, it is given the untrusted content, or its errors let the action proceed.\n## R10 G3 Misaligned-behavior review | NA: Only when deliberate misalignment is recorded as out of scope for this deployment, with a reason and approval.\nREQ: If deliberate misalignment is in scope, test a second model or pattern review against realistic evasive behavior. Record what it can and can't catch. Include what the reviewer can see: actions, arguments, and any visible reasoning. Reasoning the reviewer can't see is a stated gap. Include red-team runs by an agent that knows it is being watched. This review must not replace rule-based access and action controls that don't depend on a model's judgment. Deeper checks on intent belong in a separate AI-control program at the model level.\nNC: Destructive actions not blocked by a rule-based gate->B05 ; A swapped, drifting, or failing checker model not treated as a failure->A07 ; Chains of individually allowed actions going undetected->R09\n## R11 G1 Tested kill switch | NA: never\nREQ: Name who may stop the agent and how. The stop path must not depend on the agent's cooperation. The agent's credentials must not be able to change it. Set a maximum stop time that fits the potential harm. Run a dated drill on the current deployment. It must stop new actions, running work, and open tool streams, subscriptions, and tasks. Include an attempt by the agent to disable or delay the stop. Cover sub-agents and their children in the ecosystem drill (E09).\nNC: Stop signal not reaching sub-agents, children, or remote workers->E09 ; In-flight writes, queued actions, or unattended restarts after the stop->R12 ; No way to cut off the agent's access without cutting off the user->B04\n - R11.1: A stop control halts new model calls and new tool calls when set.\n     V: 1) Find the stop control (kill flag, `stop`, `halt`, feature flag, revoked token). 2) Check the loop checks it before every model call and tool call. 3) Answer no if it is checked only at run start, only between turns, a second loop or worker skips it, or a check error is treated as not stopped.\n - R11.2: The agent's own credentials and tools cannot set, clear, delete, or delay the stop control.\n     V: 1) Find where the stop flag is stored and who can write it. 2) Compare with the agent's credentials and tools. 3) Answer no if the agent's role can write, delete, or expire the flag, a tool can reach its store, or the agent can delay it (for example by catching the stop signal).\n - R11.3: Tool calls already running are cancelled or bounded by a timeout when the stop is set.\n     V: 1) Find the stop handler. 2) Check running tool calls are cancelled or bounded by a timeout. 3) Answer no if running calls finish with no timeout, or cancellation errors are swallowed and the call continues.\n - R11.4: The stop also closes open tool streams, subscriptions, and background tasks.\n     V: 1) Find streams, subscriptions, and background tasks (`asyncio.create_task`, goroutines, websockets, SSE). 2) Check the stop handler closes them. 3) Answer no if any is left open, detached, or untracked.\n - R11.5: Only named operator identities can set the stop control.\n     V: 1) Find who can set the stop (auth on the endpoint, IAM on the flag store). 2) Answer no if any authenticated user or service can set it with no named operator list. Answer unknown if access lives outside this repo.\n## R12 G1 Safe state after stopping | NA: never\nREQ: Decide what happens to writes in progress, queued actions, and outside side effects. Where you can't cancel something, show how you check and fix the result (reconcile) or undo it another way (compensate). No restart may happen unattended, and no orphaned work may be left able to act.\nNC: Stop path depends on the agent or has no tested stop time->R11 ; Recovery replay repeating irreversible actions or using tampered checkpoints->R14 ; Rollback restoring revoked permissions->C07\n - R12.1: For each operation type, code or config states whether it can be cancelled, completed, or only reconciled after a stop.\n     V: 1) Search code and config for a per-operation stop policy (cancel, complete, reconcile). 2) Answer no if any operation type the agent can run has no stated stop behavior.\n - R12.2: On stop, queued actions are cancelled or held, and automatic retries are turned off.\n     V: 1) Find queue and retry code (`retry`, `backoff`, queue workers). 2) Check stop cancels or holds queued items and turns retries off. 3) Answer no if retries continue after stop or queued jobs still run.\n - R12.3: Unclear or in-progress outside actions go on a tracked hold with an owner, and dependent actions are blocked until reconciled.\n     V: 1) Find handling of unclear or in-progress outside actions at stop. 2) Check they go on a tracked hold with an owner and block dependent actions. 3) Answer no if they are dropped, retried, or dependents proceed.\n - R12.4: A stopped run does not restart on its own; restart needs an operator action.\n     V: 1) Find restart logic (supervisors, `restartPolicy`, cron, auto-resume). 2) Answer no if a stopped run restarts automatically (`restartPolicy: Always`, auto-resume from checkpoint) without an operator action.\n - R12.5: Workers and queued jobs lose their credentials or authority at stop, so no orphaned work can act.\n     V: 1) Find workers and queued jobs and the credentials they hold. 2) Check stop revokes or expires them. 3) Answer no if workers keep long-lived credentials after stop, or jobs carry their own tokens that stay valid.\n## R13 G1 Full execution audit | NA: never\nREQ: Record each run as an execution graph: a linked record of every step. It must include proposed actions, tool calls and key arguments, and authorization decisions with the policy version used. It must also include denials, approvals with their IDs, results, errors, and timestamps. Rebuild a typical run from this record, including failed actions. Don't rely on the run's final answer.\nNC: Action records missing any of the six identity fields->A02 ; Agent able to erase or rewrite its own audit history->E10 ; Audit retention, access, redaction, or export rules missing->E11 ; Run can't be rebuilt across gateway, tool services, and delegated workers->E10 ; Prompt a parent gave a sub-agent not recorded->A06\n - R13.1: Each step writes a record linked to the run: proposed action, tool call with key arguments, result, error, and timestamp.\n     V: 1) Find step logging (trace spans, audit log writes). 2) Check each record has run ID, proposed action, tool call with key arguments, result, error, and timestamp. 3) Answer no if any field is missing, or logging happens only on the success branch.\n - R13.2: Authorization decisions are recorded with the policy version, including denials, not only allowed calls.\n     V: 1) Find authorization decision code. 2) Check both allow and deny are logged with the policy version. 3) Answer no if denials are not logged, or no policy version is recorded.\n - R13.3: Approved actions record their approval ID.\n     V: 1) Find where approved actions are logged. 2) Answer no if the approval ID is not recorded with the action.\n - R13.4: Tool errors, timeouts, and retries are recorded, and each operation gets an ID before it is sent, so unclear results can be matched to the destination's records.\n     V: 1) Find error, timeout, and retry handling. 2) Check each is logged and each operation gets an ID before sending. 3) Answer no if `except`/`catch` blocks skip logging, retries are not logged, or the ID is created after the response.\n - R13.5: Records are written by the harness or gateway, not by the model, and do not depend on the run's final answer.\n     V: 1) Find who writes records. 2) Answer no if the model writes them via a tool, records are built from the final answer, or logging runs only if the run completes.\n## R14 G2 Recovery integrity | NA: Only when no logs or checkpoints drive recovery and there is no automatic replay.\nREQ: If logs or checkpoints drive recovery, make them tamper-evident. Before using them, verify them against a separately protected trust anchor or signing key. Test that verification rejects changed checkpoints. It must also catch truncation, rollback to an older valid checkpoint, and replacement of a whole hash chain. For automatic recovery, confirm the destination supports idempotency: sending the same request twice has the effect of sending it once. Replay only within the destination's documented scope and time window. Confirm that actions you can't undo don't happen twice. Where support is missing or the result is unclear, check that replay pauses for reconciliation instead of sending again.\nNC: What happens to in-flight writes and queued actions at stop->R12 ; Rollback to an approved earlier release not tested->C07 ; Agent able to delete or rewrite its own audit history->E10\n - R14.1: Checkpoints and audit records used for recovery are hash-chained or signed.\n     V: 1) Find checkpoint and audit storage used for recovery. 2) Check for hash chaining or signatures. 3) Answer no if records are plain rows or files with no chain or signature.\n - R14.2: Recovery verifies them against a key or trust anchor stored separately from the records, and refuses any that fail.\n     V: 1) Find recovery code that loads checkpoints. 2) Check it verifies against a key stored separately and refuses failures. 3) Answer no if the key sits in the same store, verification is skipped, or failure is logged and recovery continues.\n - R14.3: Verification catches truncation, rollback to an older valid checkpoint, and replacement of the whole chain, for example with a signed head or sequence number held outside the store.\n     V: 1) Check verification uses a signed head or sequence number held outside the store. 2) Answer no if truncation, rollback to an older valid checkpoint, or replacing the whole chain would still pass.\n - R14.4: Automatic replay sends idempotency keys that the destination enforces, and only for integrations that support them.\n     V: 1) Find replay code. 2) Check it sends idempotency keys (`Idempotency-Key`, `client_token`) only to integrations that enforce them. 3) Answer no if replay sends without keys, uses a local dedupe table as the only guard, or replays to integrations without support.\n - R14.5: When idempotency is missing or the result is unclear, replay pauses for reconciliation instead of resending.\n     V: 1) Find replay handling for unclear results or missing idempotency. 2) Answer no if replay resends instead of pausing for reconciliation, or retry is the default on timeout.","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false}}
