{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_017zhDNfSsF9E5gm97sx4EEK","type":"tool_result","content":"9207) and blocks private, loopback, and metadata addresses (169.254.169.254) in discovery URLs. 3) Answer no if either is missing. If it is not an OAuth client, answer yes citing the config that shows that.\n## E07 G2 Untrusted peer messages | NA: The agent receives no messages from other agents (no A2A server, peer endpoint, or agent-to-agent channel).\nREQ: Authenticate the sending agent (prove its identity) on every message, even validly signed ones. Validate message types and schemas, and check what that peer is allowed to ask for. Where a replay could repeat an action, check freshness and track nonces or request IDs. Checking the sender alone doesn't stop replays. Test faked identity, replayed requests, requests across tenants, and instructions passed along by a compromised peer. Verify signed agent cards where they exist. A card says who an agent is, not what it may do. Check callback and webhook URLs against SSRF. A valid sender doesn't make a message's content trusted.\nNC: Tool server identified only by its self-reported name->E05 ; Missing schema or size checks on API responses->R01 ; Peers or children still running after a stop->E09 ; Run can't be rebuilt across gateway, tool services, and delegated workers->E10\n - E07.1: Every peer message is authenticated to a specific sending agent, even when the message is validly signed.\n     V: 1) Find the handler for incoming peer messages (A2A server, peer endpoint). 2) Check each message is authenticated to a specific sending agent (mTLS identity, verified token subject). 3) Answer no if any route accepts unauthenticated messages, trusts a self-declared sender field, or accepts any valid signature without mapping it to a known agent.\n - E07.2: Message types and schemas are validated, and each request is checked against what that peer is allowed to ask for, including tenant.\n     V: 1) Open the peer message handler. 2) Check message type and schema validation. 3) Check a per-peer allowlist of request types and tenants, keyed on the authenticated sender. 4) Answer no if schemas are unchecked, any authenticated peer can ask for anything, or tenant is taken from the message body.\n - E07.3: Action requests carry a nonce or request ID and a freshness check, and replays are rejected or do not repeat side effects.\n     V: 1) Find action request handling. 2) Check for a nonce or request ID plus a freshness (timestamp) check, with seen IDs stored. 3) Answer no if no replay check exists, the nonce is never stored or compared, or a repeated ID repeats side effects.\n - E07.4: Peer message content is handled as untrusted data and is not placed into system or instruction prompts.\n     V: 1) Trace peer message content to the prompt builder. 2) Check it goes into user or tool-result content as untrusted data. 3) Answer no if any path places it into the system prompt or an instruction template.\n - E07.5: Callback and webhook URLs are checked against SSRF, with private and metadata addresses blocked.\n     V: 1) Find code that calls callback or webhook URLs from peers. 2) Check URL validation blocks private, loopback, link-local, and metadata addresses (169.254.169.254). 3) Answer no if any callback is fetched without the check, or only the hostname text is checked and not the resolved address.\n## E08 G1 Bounded delegated authority | NA: The agent never delegates to sub-agents or peer agents.\nREQ: Give sub-agents only the powers approved for their task. Enforce each child's role and any explicit approval from a higher authority. A parent must not gain a power it was denied just by asking a more powerful peer to act. Narrow credentials at each hop, for example with token exchange or transaction tokens. Authorize based on the original subject (the user or system whose authority is used) and the current actor. Earlier actors in a delegation chain are for the record only, not a reason to grant access. Test that escalation attempt.\nNC: Approvals that can be replayed or aren't tied to exact arguments->B06 ; Prompt a parent gave a sub-agent not recorded->A06 ; Children still running after the parent is stopped->E09 ; Replayed or faked peer-agent messages->E07 ; No limit on how many sub-agents a run can start->B07\n - E08.1: Each child gets a credential narrowed to its approved task (for example by token exchange or transaction tokens), never the parent's full credential.\n     V: 1) Find where a child agent gets its credential. 2) Check it is a narrowed token for the task (token exchange, transaction token, scoped-down role). 3) Answer no if any spawn path passes the parent's token, API key, or full role, or the child inherits the parent's environment credentials.\n - E08.2: Authorization at the action uses the original subject and the current actor, and earlier actors in the delegation chain never add access.\n     V: 1) Open authorization code for delegated actions. 2) Check it decides on the original subject and the current actor. 3) Answer no if it grants access because of any earlier actor in the chain (nested `act` claims, parent identity) or a union of chain permissions.\n - E08.3: An action the parent was denied is also denied when routed through a more powerful peer, unless it carries its own explicit approval.\n     V: 1) Find how a parent asks a peer or child to act. 2) Check the request carries the parent's limits and the peer re-checks them, or requires its own explicit approval. 3) Answer no if a peer acts with its own wider permissions on a parent's request without that check.\n - E08.4: Downstream services reject the parent's token when the child presents it (audience-bound tokens).\n     V: 1) Find how child tokens are issued and how downstream services validate tokens. 2) Check tokens are audience-bound and services verify `aud`. 3) Answer no if audience is unset, a wildcard, or not checked. Answer unknown if downstream services are outside this repo.\n## E09 G1 Recursive shutdown | NA: never\nREQ: Run a drill across a parent, nested children, remote workers, queued work, and open tool streams, subscriptions, and tasks, where they exist. Check that the stop reaches every descendant within the set time. It must revoke access as needed and prevent orphaned retries or restarts. Record how outside effects that already happened are reconciled.\nNC: Agent able to disable or delay its own stop->R11 ; No way to cut off the agent's access without cutting off the user->B04 ; No drill for a compromised shared tool or agent type->E12 ; Sub-agent given powers beyond its task->E08\n - E09.1: A stop signal from the root reaches every descendant: nested children, remote workers, queued work, and open tool streams, subscriptions, and tasks.\n     V: 1) Find the stop handler. 2) Check it reaches nested children, remote workers, queued jobs, and open tool streams, subscriptions, and tasks (cancel calls, MCP `notifications/cancelled`). 3) Answer no if any of these that exists in the code is not reached, or stop only sets an in-process flag that remote workers never read.\n - E09.2: Stopping revokes descendants' credentials or leases, and leases or tokens are short-lived, so a disconnected worker loses authority within the stop time limit.\n     V: 1) Open the stop path and credential issuing code. 2) Check stopping revokes descendants' tokens or leases. 3) Check token or lease lifetime fits within the stop time limit. 4) Answer no if no revocation exists, or tokens are long-lived so a disconnected worker keeps authority.\n - E09.3: Queues and schedulers refuse new work, retries, and restarts for a stopped tree.\n     V: 1) Find queue consumers, schedulers, and retry logic. 2) Check each looks up the stopped state before running new work, retries, or restarts. 3) Answer no if any consumer, retry decorator, or restart policy runs without that check.\n - E09.4: Outside effects already completed are recorded for reconciliation.\n     V: 1) Open the stop path. 2) Check it records outside effects already completed (tool calls, writes, sent messages) for reconciliation. 3) Answer no if nothing is recorded, or only in-flight work is listed.\n## E10 G1 Audit across service boundaries | NA: never\nREQ: Rebuild a run across the gateway, tool services, and delegated workers, with all six identity fields kept. Check that the agent can't erase or rewrite its audit history, either directly or by asking a more powerful tool or peer. Check that a vendor boundary doesn't leave any action untraceable to the agent and instance that did it.\nNC: Audit retention, access, redaction, or export rules missing->E11 ; Authorization decisions missing the policy version in the run record->R13 ; Instance or tenant labels taken from what the model supplies->A04 ; Recovery checkpoints not tamper-evident->R14\n - E10.1: The gateway, tool services, and delegated workers each write audit records with all six identity fields and the shared trace context.\n     V: 1) Find audit writers in the gateway, tool services, and delegated workers. 2) Check each writes all six identity fields and the shared trace context. 3) Answer no if any component writes records without them or starts a new trace. Answer unknown if a component's code is outside this repo.\n - E10.2: The agent's credentials have no permission to delete, update, or overwrite audit records (for example an append-only or WORM store with IAM denies).\n     V: 1) Find the agent's IAM role or database grants and the audit store config. 2) Check the agent has no delete, update, or overwrite rights (`DeleteObject`, overwriting `PutObject`, `UPDATE`, `DELETE`, `TRUNCATE`) and the store is append-only or WORM (Object Lock). 3) Answer no if the agent's credentials can delete or rewrite records, or grant `*`. Answer unknown if these are set outside this repo.\n - E10.3: No tool or peer the agent can call is able to delete or rewrite audit records on its behalf, or such requests are held for approval under B05.\n     V: 1) List the tools and peers the agent can call. 2) For each, check whether it can delete or rewrite audit records (database admin, log management, storage tools). 3) Answer no if any can and such requests are not held for approval under B05.\n - E10.4: Calls into vendor services are mapped from the gateway's verified identity and correlation ID to the vendor's job or receipt ID.\n     V: 1) Find calls into vendor services. 2) Check the gateway stores its verified identity and correlation ID together with the vendor's job or receipt ID. 3) Answer no if the vendor ID is not stored, or stored without the link to identity and correlation ID.\n## E11 G2 Protect shared evidence | NA: never\nREQ: Set rules for how long audit records are kept, who can see them, what is redacted, how their integrity is checked, and how they are exported and restored. Test pulling up a past run, and test that missing records or a broken pipeline get noticed. Keep records long enough for investigations and for the law. For example, the EU AI Act requires providers and deployers of high-risk systems to keep automatically generated logs for at least six months (Articles 19 and 26). High-impact actions must fail closed when required audit capture isn't working.\nNC: Agent able to erase or rewrite its own audit history->E10 ; Run record missing denials, approvals, or results->R13 ; Secrets left in tool output, responses, or normal logs->R03\n - E11.1: Audit retention is set in config to cover the investigation window and the legal minimum (for example at least six months for EU AI Act high-risk systems).\n     V: 1) Find retention settings for audit storage: bucket lifecycle rules, log retention days, TTLs. 2) Check the value covers the investigation window and the legal minimum (for example six months for EU AI Act high-risk systems). 3) Answer no if retention is shorter or unset. Answer unknown if audit storage is configured outside this repo.\n - E11.2: Access to audit records is limited by role, and redaction rules apply to sensitive fields.\n     V: 1) Find IAM policies or ACLs on audit storage. 2) Check reads are limited by role. 3) Check redaction rules apply to sensitive fields. 4) Answer no if read access is broad (`*`, all engineers) or no redaction exists.\n - E11.3: Integrity protection (hash chain, signatures, or WORM) plus sequence numbers or delivery receipts let missing records be detected, with an alert on gaps or pipeline failure.\n     V: 1) Open the audit writer and store config. 2) Check for integrity protection (hash chain, signatures, or WORM) plus sequence numbers or delivery receipts. 3) Check an alert fires on gaps or pipeline failure. 4) Answer no if any piece is missing, or a hash is computed but never verified.\n - E11.4: High-impact actions are blocked when required audit capture is down.\n     V: 1) Find high-impact action paths. 2) Check they confirm audit capture is working before the action runs. 3) Answer no if audit write errors are caught and the action proceeds, or the audit record is written only after success.\n## E12 G3 Fleet incident response | NA: never\nREQ: Run a drill where a shared tool or agent type is compromised. Find the affected instances and tenants. Quarantine the dependency, revoke the access involved, stop affected work, and alert the named responders. Check that alerts about abuse across agents, and about budgets, reach the team that can act. Name who decides whether to report outside the organization and share information.\nNC: Kill switch for a single agent not drilled->R11 ; No owner named on each side of a shared control->E01 ; Tool or MCP server approved without vetting or pinning->E02 ; Budget and rate limits not enforced outside the model->B07","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e7e91a56-8e26-4882-b939-10679f6422bd","uuid":"95a1439f-7b6f-4ea3-8ca2-614ae72680e1","timestamp":"2026-10-0